# Antipodal grasp candidates for one object, computed from the WRIST camera's
# point cloud at whatever look pose the arm is currently holding. Deliberately a
# SERVICE, not a topic: a topic's newest message can be stale by the time it is
# acted on, which is the failure mode that produced the label-swap / wrong-target
# bugs in this stack. Here the caller gets geometry computed from frames captured
# DURING its own call, or an explicit failure.
#
# Implemented by reachability_gng/grasp_sampler.py -- see that node's docstring
# for the algorithm and for what each score actually measures.

# Which object. Preferred form is "#<tid>", a persistent object_localizer track
# id from /detected_objects/tracks -- it names the same physical object across
# calls. A bare index into the live /detected_objects PoseArray is accepted for
# parity with gantry_reach_executor's ~/pick and ~/look, but that array's length
# and order change between publishes, so an index can name a different object
# than the caller meant (observed live). "" falls back to /target_object.
string target

# Per-call overrides; <= 0 means "use the node's parameter".
float64 roi                     # ROI crop radius (m) about the reported object
int32 captures                  # wrist cloud frames to accumulate
int32 max_candidates            # cap on how many grasps to return
---
bool success
string message

# Grasp poses, BEST FIRST. header.frame_id is the world frame.
#
# Each pose is for the GRIPPER BASE LINK (e.g. t1_a1_gripper_base_link), at the
# CLOSED-ON-OBJECT position -- i.e. the finger midpoint sits on the contact
# midpoint. It is NOT a pre-grasp/stand-off pose; the caller backs off along the
# approach axis by however much it wants. Orientation follows the gripper's own
# measured axes: local +Z is the approach direction (points at the fingertips)
# and local Y is the finger-spread axis (so the fingers close along `widths`).
geometry_msgs/PoseArray grasps

# Per-grasp, index-aligned with grasps.poses.
float64[] scores                # combined rank score, descending
float64[] widths                # contact separation (m) = pad gap the grasp needs
float64[] antipodal             # antipodality alone, 1.0 = perfectly opposed normals
float64[] gravity               # gravity alignment alone, 1.0 = closing axis horizontal

# True when BOTH contacts of this grasp have real observed surface support, so
# the grasp does not rest on an inferred/unseen far side. A false here on the
# top candidate is the escalation trigger for an oblique second look.
bool[] both_sides_observed

# Diagnostics -- enough to tell "bad geometry" from "bad input" without RViz.
int32 n_cluster_points          # points in the chosen object cluster
int32 n_gripper_filtered        # points dropped as the robot's own hand
int32 n_support_filtered        # points dropped as the support surface
geometry_msgs/Vector3 principal_axis    # cluster's PCA principal axis (world)
geometry_msgs/Vector3 cluster_extent    # cluster size (m) along its 3 PCA axes
geometry_msgs/Point cluster_centroid    # cluster centroid (world)
